The writer's key concerns are:
(i) whether time and modern 'improvements' have spoilt the place - 'I wondered how time would have marred this unique, this beautiful spot';
(ii) whether the tarred road has 'found it out' and 'in what other ways it would be desolated';
(iii) the passing of time and his own ageing, felt as the 'creepy' sense of living a 'dual existence' in which he is both himself and his father;
(iv) the loss of the lake's quiet, wild quality to outboard motors and to crowds of campers.
Underlying all four is the larger concern for the preservation of nature.
